WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.817
A system asks for inputs that satisfy several equations at the same time

00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:13.317
These lines represent two different rules, and their shared point has coordinates that make both equalities true simultaneously

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:17.067
Nearby points on just one line do not qualify.

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:23.772
Because both expressions equal y at the intersection, set them equal to each other

00:00:23.772 --> 00:00:29.950
Add x and subtract one to get three x equals six, then find x equals two

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.267
Substituting back gives y equals five.

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:36.161
Different systems can have no solution

00:00:36.161 --> 00:00:42.215
These lines have equal rates but different vertical starting values, so they remain separated

00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:49.133
Setting their expressions equal cancels the x terms and leaves the impossible statement one equals four.

00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:56.977
If one equation is simply a nonzero multiple of the other, both describe the same line

00:00:56.977 --> 00:01:00.250
Every point on that line satisfies the system

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:05.567
Thus two linear equations can have one solution, no solutions, or infinitely many.
